<!-- ================== SCIENCE OF HABIT REPLACEMENT ================== -->
<section class="science" id="science-habit">
  <div class="science-inner">
    <div class="science-head">
      <div>
        <div class="section-eyebrow">The research layer</div>
        <h2 class="section-h2">
          How habits <span class="italic-word">actually</span><br>
          change — in four parts.
        </h2>
      </div>
      <div class="science-head-right">
        <p>
          Lio isn't magic. It's a physical object designed around what behavioral science already knows about <strong>cue, routine, reward,</strong> the role of breath, and the emotional weight of scent. The research below is what the design is built on.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="mobile-swipe-hint">Swipe</div>
    <div class="science-grid">

      <div class="science-card">
        <div class="science-card-num">
          <div class="science-card-num-circle">01</div>
          <div class="science-card-num-label">The habit loop</div>
        </div>
        <h3 class="science-card-h">Habits run on <span class="italic-word">cue, routine, reward</span> — not willpower.</h3>
        <p class="science-card-body">
          Decades of neuroscience show habits form in the <strong>basal ganglia</strong> as three-part loops: a cue triggers a routine, a routine delivers a reward. Once the loop is wired, it runs automatically. You don't decide to do it — you notice you've done it.
        </p>
        <div class="science-card-cite">
          <a href="https://thedecisionlab.com/reference-guide/psychology/car-model" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Duhigg, <em>The Power of Habit</em></a> (2012) · drawing on Graybiel et al., MIT McGovern Institute.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="science-card">
        <div class="science-card-num">
          <div class="science-card-num-circle">02</div>
          <div class="science-card-num-label">Substitution, not erasure</div>
        </div>
        <h3 class="science-card-h">You don't break a loop. You <span class="italic-word">swap</span> the middle.</h3>
        <p class="science-card-body">
          The research-supported approach to changing habits is not to erase them but to <strong>keep the cue and the reward, and substitute a new routine</strong> that delivers comparable satisfaction. Removing cues alone doesn't extinguish a craving — a replacement routine is what actually closes the loop.
        </p>
        <div class="science-card-cite">
          <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17469900/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wood &amp; Neal, <em>Psychological Review</em></a> (2007) · habit–goal interface and routine substitution.
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="science-card">
        <div class="science-card-num">
          <div class="science-card-num-circle">03</div>
          <div class="science-card-num-label">Breath as an anchor</div>
        </div>
        <h3 class="science-card-h">A slower breath shifts your <span class="italic-word">state</span> in minutes.</h3>
        <p class="science-card-body">
          A systematic review of slow breathing (≤6 breaths/min) across 31 studies found significant increases in <strong>parasympathetic activity and heart-rate variability,</strong> with reductions in perceived anxiety after sessions as short as 5 minutes. A deliberate inhale is one of the fastest physiological resets available.
        </p>
        <div class="science-card-cite">
          <a href="https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6137615/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zaccaro et al., <em>Frontiers in Human Neuroscience</em></a> (2018) · also Magnon et al., <em>Scientific Reports</em> (2021).
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="science-card">
        <div class="science-card-num">
          <div class="science-card-num-circle">04</div>
          <div class="science-card-num-label">Scent is direct</div>
        </div>
        <h3 class="science-card-h">Smell skips the <span class="italic-word">filter.</span></h3>
        <p class="science-card-body">
          Olfactory signals are the only sensory input that reaches the amygdala and hippocampus <strong>without passing through the thalamus first.</strong> That anatomical shortcut is why scent triggers emotion and memory faster and more vividly than sight or sound — and why a flavored inhale is an unusually efficient cue for a new routine.
        </p>
        <div class="science-card-cite">
          <a href="https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.94.8.4119" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Zald &amp; Pardo, <em>PNAS</em></a> (1997) · amygdala activation during olfactory processing. Review: <a href="https://magazine.hms.harvard.edu/articles/connections-between-smell-memory-and-health" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Harvard Medicine Magazine</a> (2023).
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <p class="science-foot">
      All citations are peer-reviewed or editorially reviewed sources. Lio is designed around these principles. It is a wellness and aromatherapy product, not a medical device — no therapeutic, cessation, or disease-related claims are made.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

